# Canonical origin of this deployment.
#
# Used for the Supabase sign-in redirect, which must be absolute. Left unset the
# origin is taken from the incoming request, which works for local and preview
# deployments, but Supabase's dashboard also needs the production URL allowed in
# its redirect list — this value is what belongs there.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase (server-side only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The Vercel integration provides these twice: once as `SUPABASE_*` and once as
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_*`. Only the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` pair is inlined into the
# browser bundle, permanently and unavoidably.
#
# Nothing here needs a browser-side Supabase client. Owner sign-in is requested
# from a Route Handler so the allowlist is checked *before* any email is sent,
# and the session is read from a server component. So the `SUPABASE_` pair is
# sufficient on its own and the client bundle holds no Supabase credential.
#
# The `POSTGRES_*` variables the integration also sets are not used: this project
# talks to Supabase over its REST API and has no Prisma, no ORM and no direct
# database connection. They can stay in the dashboard.
#
# **Set this to the project base, with no `/rest/v1`.** Supabase does not serve
# every API from one path, and this one variable covers two of them:
#
#   auth, realtime, storage   /auth/v1   /realtime/v1   /storage/v1
#   PostgREST (the tables)    /rest/v1
#
# `supabase-js` resolves all of those off the single project URL, so it has to be
# the bare host. Every table read and write here goes through `fetch` instead —
# see `supabaseRestBase` in `src/infrastructure/supabase/server.ts`, which derives
# the PostgREST base from this value.
#
# **Do not add `/rest/v1` to this variable.** The hosted gateway serves the tables
# under it and answers the bare host with `{"error":"requested path is
# invalid"}` — a 404, not a 401, so nothing says the credential is wrong, and
# every table 404s including ones that have worked for months. It reads the other
# way too: with the path on this variable, `supabase-js` derives
# `/rest/rest/v1` and owner sign-in, realtime and the magic-link email break.
#
# That mistake was invisible in both directions, which is why the fallbacks made
# it expensive: the résumé serves the versioned catalog and the blog serves its
# versioned articles, so the site looks entirely healthy while the database holds
# the owner's edits. `/admin` now reports which one served the page.
#
# **The local stack in `docker-compose.supabase.yml` is the opposite.** It runs
# PostgREST on its own port with nothing in front of it, so its tables live at the
# **root** — `http://127.0.0.1:54330`, and `/rest/v1` there returns 404.
# `supabaseRestBase` leaves a loopback `http` URL alone, which is how one rule
# serves both deployments. `npm run db:keys` writes that value into `.env.local`.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# Optional. Only needed when PostgREST is not at `/rest/v1` and not at the root
# of a loopback host — a self-hosted Supabase behind a proxy, for instance.
# Used **verbatim**, never passed through the derivation above, because setting it
# means you have already looked at where your tables are mounted.
# SUPABASE_REST_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co/rest/v1

# Bypasses row level security. Server-side only. Supabase renamed this from
# `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`; same privilege, current name.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=server-only-secret-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owner authentication (passwordless)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `/admin` is restricted to the addresses listed here, and nothing else. The
# check is exact address matching, not a substring test, and when ADMIN_EMAIL is
# empty NOBODY is authorised — access fails closed.
#
# More than one owner? Comma separate them: you@example.com,other@example.com
ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Development only, and only under `next dev`. Opens /admin with no sign-in link,
# no session and no mail provider, which is what makes the owner area usable
# locally at all — the Resend adapter also needs RESEND_FROM, so without this the
# local admin page cannot get past the form.
#
# Two things refuse it: a Vercel production or preview, and any build that is not a
# development server. The second one is not redundant — Next loads `.env.local` over
# the process environment, so on a laptop this flag would otherwise open the owner
# area under `next build && next start`, where no Vercel variable exists to stop it.
# Keep it in `.env.local`, which is git-ignored, and not here.
#
# It also refuses every build that is not `next dev` — `NODE_ENV` must be
# `development` — because Next loads `.env.local` over the process environment, so a
# laptop with this on would otherwise open the owner area under `next build && next
# start` with no platform variable to stop it.
#
# Accepted: 1, true, yes, on. Anything else reads as off.
ADMIN_AUTH_BYPASS=off

# Optional. A reader of the database can see which elements were clicked; the
# Vercel Supabase integration provisions the `click_aggregates` table if you
# apply the migration. Without it the panel answers from a process-local counter
# and the numbers are not shared between instances.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email provider — the owner sign-in link
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Something has to send the one-time link, and the two variables below are what
# let it. BOTH are required, and the API key alone is not half a working setup:
# with either one missing the adapter reports itself unconfigured, the sign-in
# request answers 503 `email_not_configured`, and `/admin` says the mail
# provider is not reachable from this deployment. A configuration error is named
# as one here, rather than quietly falling back to a sender that would bounce.

# `resend`, or `supabase` to choose the other one — trimmed and read
# case-insensitively, so `SUPABASE` works. `supabase` is the pre-ADR-009 path,
# kept as a rollback: it delivers through Supabase's own SMTP, needs no
# credentials of its own, and ignores the translated subject and body in favour
# of the dashboard's email template.
#
# Leave it out, or leave it empty, and you get the default. A value that is
# neither adapter is refused rather than guessed at — a typo must not leave a
# deployment running an adapter nobody chose. See docs/supabase-setup.md.
EMAIL_SENDER=resend

# The Resend API key, from the Resend dashboard. Server-side only, and
# deliberately not `NEXT_PUBLIC_`: a Route Handler reads it and sends it as a
# bearer token to `api.resend.com`, while Next inlines every `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
# value into the browser bundle at build time — permanently, and to everyone who
# loads the site. A delivery key inlined there is handed to every reader.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_your-api-key

# The sender, on a domain verified in the Resend dashboard under Domains. The
# accepted form is `Name <address@your-domain>`, e.g.
# `Marcelo Sandroni <no-reply@your-domain>`.
#
# No safe default is possible here, which is why this line has no placeholder
# that could be mistaken for a working one: Resend sends only from a domain the
# account has verified, so any invented address names a domain nobody controls
# and fails at the provider instead of at boot. Absent, the adapter cannot send
# and says exactly that.
#
# Resend does provide a testing sender, `onboarding@resend.dev`, and it is
# enough to prove the key, the route and the sign-in flow work before a domain
# is verified — but it delivers only to the address on the Resend account, so
# it is a way to check the wiring, not a sender to keep.
RESEND_FROM=Marcelino Sandroni <no-reply@marcelinosandroni>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Feature flags — what the site offers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both are OFF unless set to `1`, `true`, `yes` or `on`. Anything else — empty,
# a typo, `no`, `off` — reads as off, so a half-finished line here disables the
# feature instead of enabling it by accident.
#
# No `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix, on purpose. Every component that reads these is a
# server component, so the prefix would buy nothing — and it would cost the
# feature: Next inlines `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` at build time, which turns a flag into a
# value frozen into an artefact. Change it in the Vercel dashboard, redeploy, and
# it takes effect. With the plain name the value is read when the page renders,
# and it never reaches the browser bundle.
#
# WhatsApp. The phone number appears in four places — the hero's link row, the
# contact section's primary button and channel list, the resume footer, and the
# site footer (both the contact line and the link columns) — and all of them are
# gated together. Off means no `wa.me` link anywhere, not a hidden button over a
# live one. The email brief is never gated: a contact section with no way to
# make contact is not the intent.
#
# Two strings of contact copy name WhatsApp, so the section also carries
# no-WhatsApp variants of its narrative and status line. Those are new copy and
# are marked TODO(review) in `src/infrastructure/content/home/home-pt-br.ts` —
# worth reading before this ships with the flag on, because with it off they are
# the only contact copy there is.
#
# Turning this on publishes a personal phone number to anyone who loads the page.
# That is a deliberate act, so it takes an explicit value.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_WHATSAPP=off

# Resume download. Off means both the button and the endpoint are gone: the
# button is not rendered, and `GET /api/resume/{locale}/pdf` answers 404 before
# it validates the locale or compiles anything. The live-resume page still reads
# in full on the site; this controls distributing it as a file.
#
# Worth knowing: the PDFs are also published to `public/artifacts` by the
# `compile-pdf` workflow, which runs on tags and is not gated by this variable.
# If the point is "not downloadable", that path needs a look too.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_RESUME_DOWNLOAD=off

# Where the recruiter PDF's content comes from.
#
# `fixed`   - serves `public/artifacts/fixed/resume-fixed-pt-BR.pdf`, a document
#             designed and fixed in this repository and pre-rendered into a PDF.
#             The endpoint becomes a file read: no database round trip and no
#             LaTeX container on the path a recruiter's download takes.
# `database` - the previous pipeline: content read from Supabase, rendered
#             through LaTeX, with the stack profiles narrowing it per request.
#
# It has to be pre-rendered rather than rendered per request because converting
# that design needs a browser, and a browser does not fit in a serverless
# function. Regenerate the artifact with `npm run pdf:fixed`, which asserts two
# pages.
#
# Two consequences of `fixed`, both deliberate and both visible rather than
# silent: the résumé page hides the stack selector, because narrowing works on
# the relation graph and a fixed document has none; and a `?stack=` request
# answers 400 instead of returning the generic résumé under a filename that
# promises a React + Node variant.
#
# `en-US` has no fixed artifact and keeps using the database, whatever this says -
# the fixed document exists only in the language it was designed in, and serving
# the Portuguese one to an English reader would be worse than serving the
# catalog.
#
# An unrecognised value falls back to `database`, so a typo does not silently
# switch a recruiter's document.
# RESUME_PDF_SOURCE=fixed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Testimonials — named references
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Off by default, and the reason is sharper than for the two above: the table
# ships **empty**. Turning this on does not make the section appear — it allows
# the section to appear for quotes you have actually entered.
#
# A testimonial is a named person saying something true about working with you.
# Nothing on this site invents one, so enabling this before you have a quote
# changes nothing at all, which is the correct outcome rather than a gap.
#
# Add them from `/admin`. To start, ask a former CTO or head of engineering —
# one sentence is enough, and the attribution is the part that carries weight.
#
# Accepted: 1, true, yes, on. Anything else reads as off.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TESTIMONIALS=off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The direct line — whether a stranger can start a conversation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Off means the visitor waits. A chat widget does not exist on this site at all
# until you open a conversation for that browser from `/admin` → "Who is
# reading". Nothing appears, nothing is stored, and a message sent straight at
# the API is refused.
#
# On means the site is the inviter. Every visitor gets the notification in the
# corner, the four lines are typed into the terminal, and their first word opens
# the conversation. The rate limit still applies — four messages a minute, the
# same number as before — but it bounds the *rate*, not the volume: what changes
# is that a stranger can put words in your inbox without asking first.
#
# So: off is the default, and turning it on is the decision, not the absence of
# one. What it does *not* relax is closing — a conversation you have closed stays
# closed, which is the one lever that keeps working when the invitation is open.
#
# Server-side only, for the same reason as the email section: the browser never
# reads it. `/api/presence` answers whether a chat is offered and the component
# renders the answer, so the variable has no browser-side job. The database keeps
# its own copy of the rule independently — `append_visitor_message` still refuses
# a conversation nobody opened, and it is granted to `anon`, so an anonymous
# browser cannot route around this by calling Supabase directly.
#
# Accepted: 1, true, yes, on. Anything else reads as off.
CHAT_INVITATION=off
